{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794543806430,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdb5fd3ffejln5isXnr1g1SL","part":{"partID":"prt_1d325667f001Kni0lY8oUEyKuP","sessionID":"ses_e2cdb5fd3ffejln5isXnr1g1SL","messageID":"msg_1d3254b5c0014a3fMVxosg90fs","type":"tool","id":"call_842e0d9cd75f47989c69364e","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794543806079,"end":1794543806430}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794543806437,"sessionID":"ses_e2cdb5fd3ffejln5isXnr1g1SL","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3256504001WgfXlxPyYsyZp7","sessionID":"ses_e2cdb5fd3ffejln5isXnr1g1SL","messageID":"msg_1d3254b5c0014a3fMVxosg90fs","type":"tool","id":"call_9348362503fd4a84b30f1d33","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\"\n3: description: \"Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"reversibility ladder\"\n6:   - \"rollback sentence\"\n7:   - \"stakes read\"\n8:   - \"stop for a yes\"\n9:   - \"irreversible\"\n10:   - \"force push\"\n11:   - \"rewrite history\"\n12:   - \"delete overwrite migrate deploy\"\n13:   - \"sending is publishing\"\n14:   - \"overwriting an untracked file\"\n15:   - \"persistence boundaries\"\n16:   - \"who still speaks the old contract\"\n17:   - \"installing is a mutation\"\n18:   - \"reversible decisions are cheap\"\n19:   - \"decision velocity\"\n20:   - \"name the rollback first\"\n21:   - \"irreversible change\"\n22:   - \"rollback plan\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.4\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the action, not after it.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Delete, overwrite, truncate, migrate, deploy, publish, send, or install.\n36: - Force-push, rewrite history, touch branches, tags, or reflogs.\n37: - Change a shared contract: API shape, schema, serialized format, config key, exported signature.\n38: - Touch authentication, authorization, secrets, billing, or persisted user data.\n39: - Any call that leaves this machine.\n40: \n41: ## The rule\n42: \n43: **Size the effort to what the change can break. Never take an irreversible step without\n44: a written rollback and an explicit yes.**\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. OPEN WITH A STAKES READ\n49: \n50: One line, before the work: *\"Low blast, reversible: one file, no callers outside the\n51: module.\"* / *\"High blast: touches auth middleware and persisted session rows.\n52: Irreversible once migrated.\"*\n53: \n54: It makes you notice the migration is one-way before you write it, and tells the operator\n55: what they are approving.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. THE REVERSIBILITY LADDER\n60: \n61: | Tier | Examples | What it needs |\n62: |------|----------|---------------|\n63: | **Trivially reversible** | Working-tree edit to a tracked file; a new untracked file; a local commit | Proceed |\n64: | **Reversible with effort** | A migration with a working down step; a regenerable artifact; an installed dependency | Say what undoing costs, then proceed; an install still waits for a yes (`AGENTS.md` §3) |\n65: | **Irreversible** | Sent message, comment, or email; published package or release; deleted remote data; an overwritten or deleted untracked file; rewritten shared history; force-push; **any push to a non-allowlisted remote branch**; an external call with side effects; a destructive migration with no down step | **Written rollback + explicit yes, first** |\n66: \n67: Two traps sit in the middle tier and behave like the bottom one:\n68: \n69: - **Overwriting or deleting an untracked or ignored file** is irreversible; git holds no\n70:   copy. Look at the target before writing over it or removing it, always.\n71:   This does not cover runtime lock or state files a running workflow creates and regenerates itself, such as `.deep-review.lock`.\n72: - **Sending is publishing.** Content that reaches an external service may be cached,\n73:   logged, or indexed even if you delete it a second later.\n74: \n75: The tiers cut both ways. The bottom tier says stop and ask; **the top tier says decide\n76: and move on.** A change that reverts in one line does not earn a paragraph of\n77: deliberation, a comparison table, or a question to the operator. Pick the option you\n78: would defend, mark the spot if the choice is non-obvious, and keep going.\n79: \n80: The failure this prevents is the mirror of the one below: not a risky step taken without\n81: a rollback, but a cheap step stalled at a fork this table has already priced as free.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. THE ROLLBACK SENTENCE\n86: \n87: Before any tier-2 or tier-3 action, write it out: **\"To undo this: ___\"**; the command,\n88: the backup path, the revert commit, the down migration. If you cannot complete that\n89: sentence with something real, **you are not ready to act**; that is the finding, and it\n90: goes to the operator.\n91: \n92: At tier 3, **stop and wait for a yes.** Not \"I'll proceed unless you object.\"\n93: \n94: **Before you stop, finish what the yes does not depend on.** Do every reversible step\n95: first, so the operator approves a finished, reviewable result and the yes is the last\n96: step.\n97: \n98: **Approval does not transfer.** A yes for one destructive action does not cover the next\n99: one, a later one of the same kind, or a wider version of the same one. Each push to a\n100: non-allowlisted remote branch needs its own in-the-moment yes.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 4. WHO STILL SPEAKS THE OLD CONTRACT\n105: \n106: Before changing anything shared, enumerate what was built against the old shape:\n107: \n108: - Deployed servers and running processes still on the old code.\n109: - Installed clients, SDKs, and other repositories that pin this interface.\n110: - Caches, queues, and in-flight messages holding the old format.\n111: - **Persisted data written by the old code:** the most-missed one.\n112: - Saved configuration, environment files, and CI definitions.\n113: - Documentation and examples that will now be wrong.\n114: \n115: Name the ones that exist here. \"No other callers\" is a claim under\n116: `evidence-and-proof.md`, grep for it, do not assume it.\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 5. PERSISTENCE BOUNDARIES\n121: \n122: **Removing a file from the working tree is not eradicating its content.** These are\n123: different jobs and the operator means one of them:\n124: \n125: | Boundary | Where the content still lives |\n126: |----------|-------------------------------|\n127: | Working tree | the file itself |\n128: | Local repository | index, stashes, prior commits, reflog |\n129: | Remote | pushed branches, tags, PRs, forks, CI logs |\n130: | Derived | build artifacts, caches, coverage reports, bundles |\n131: | External | logs, backups, monitoring, anything already sent |\n132: \n133: Inventory every location that applies, then **keep ordinary removal scoped to the\n134: surface that was asked for**. Do not rewrite history, branches, or reflogs to \"finish\n135: the job\" until the rollback is written and the operator has approved that specific\n136: destructive step. If a secret was committed, say so plainly, rotation is the real\n137: remedy and it is the operator's to run.\n138: \n139: ---\n140: \n141: ## 6. INSTALLING IS A MUTATION\n142: \n143: A dependency install changes the environment, the lockfile, and the trust surface, and\n144: it is the one mutation that feels like a read. Same gates as any other change: in scope,\n145: is there an existing tool, what is the rollback. Prefer what the project already has.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 7. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Placed the action on the reversibility ladder and said so.\n152: - [ ] Looked at the target before deleting or overwriting it.\n153: - [ ] The rollback sentence is written and concrete.\n154: - [ ] For tier 3, I have an explicit, in-the-moment yes for *this* action.\n155: - [ ] Enumerated what still speaks the old contract, by searching rather than assuming.\n156: - [ ] Removal is scoped to the requested surface; nothing wider happened silently.\n157: - [ ] Cheap, reversible forks were decided rather than deliberated.\n158: - [ ] Any installation went through the same scope, approval and verification as any other mutation.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\"\n3: description: \"Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"reversibility ladder\"\n6:   - \"rollback sentence\"\n7:   - \"stakes read\"\n8:   - \"stop for a yes\"\n9:   - \"irreversible\"\n10:   - \"force push\"\n11:   - \"rewrite history\"\n12:   - \"delete overwrite migrate deploy\"\n13:   - \"sending is publishing\"\n14:   - \"overwriting an untracked file\"\n15:   - \"persistence boundaries\"\n16:   - \"who still speaks the old contract\"\n17:   - \"installing is a mutation\"\n18:   - \"reversible decisions are cheap\"\n19:   - \"decision velocity\"\n20:   - \"name the rollback first\"\n21:   - \"irreversible change\"\n22:   - \"rollback plan\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.4\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Blast radius and reversibility\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the action, not after it.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - Delete, overwrite, truncate, migrate, deploy, publish, send, or install.\n36: - Force-push, rewrite history, touch branches, tags, or reflogs.\n37: - Change a shared contract: API shape, schema, serialized format, config key, exported signature.\n38: - Touch authentication, authorization, secrets, billing, or persisted user data.\n39: - Any call that leaves this machine.\n40: \n41: ## The rule\n42: \n43: **Size the effort to what the change can break. Never take an irreversible step without\n44: a written rollback and an explicit yes.**\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. OPEN WITH A STAKES READ\n49: \n50: One line, before the work: *\"Low blast, reversible: one file, no callers outside the\n51: module.\"* / *\"High blast: touches auth middleware and persisted session rows.\n52: Irreversible once migrated.\"*\n53: \n54: It makes you notice the migration is one-way before you write it, and tells the operator\n55: what they are approving.\n56: \n57: ---\n58: \n59: ## 2. THE REVERSIBILITY LADDER\n60: \n61: | Tier | Examples | What it needs |\n62: |------|----------|---------------|\n63: | **Trivially reversible** | Working-tree edit to a tracked file; a new untracked file; a local commit | Proceed |\n64: | **Reversible with effort** | A migration with a working down step; a regenerable artifact; an installed dependency | Say what undoing costs, then proceed; an install still waits for a yes (`AGENTS.md` §3) |\n65: | **Irreversible** | Sent message, comment, or email; published package or release; deleted remote data; an overwritten or deleted untracked file; rewritten shared history; force-push; **any push to a non-allowlisted remote branch**; an external call with side effects; a destructive migration with no down step | **Written rollback + explicit yes, first** |\n66: \n67: Two traps sit in the middle tier and behave like the bottom one:\n68: \n69: - **Overwriting or deleting an untracked or ignored file** is irreversible; git holds no\n70:   copy. Look at the target before writing over it or removing it, always.\n71:   This does not cover runtime lock or state files a running workflow creates and regenerates itself, such as `.deep-review.lock`.\n72: - **Sending is publishing.** Content that reaches an external service may be cached,\n73:   logged, or indexed even if you delete it a second later.\n74: \n75: The tiers cut both ways. The bottom tier says stop and ask; **the top tier says decide\n76: and move on.** A change that reverts in one line does not earn a paragraph of\n77: deliberation, a comparison table, or a question to the operator. Pick the option you\n78: would defend, mark the spot if the choice is non-obvious, and keep going.\n79: \n80: The failure this prevents is the mirror of the one below: not a risky step taken without\n81: a rollback, but a cheap step stalled at a fork this table has already priced as free.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. THE ROLLBACK SENTENCE\n86: \n87: Before any tier-2 or tier-3 action, write it out: **\"To undo this: ___\"**; the command,\n88: the backup path, the revert commit, the down migration. If you cannot complete that\n89: sentence with something real, **you are not ready to act**; that is the finding, and it\n90: goes to the operator.\n91: \n92: At tier 3, **stop and wait for a yes.** Not \"I'll proceed unless you object.\"\n93: \n94: **Before you stop, finish what the yes does not depend on.** Do every reversible step\n95: first, so the operator approves a finished, reviewable result and the yes is the last\n96: step.\n97: \n98: **Approval does not transfer.** A yes for one destructive action does not cover the next\n99: one, a later one of the same kind, or a wider version of the same one. Each push to a\n100: non-allowlisted remote branch needs its own in-the-moment yes.\n101: \n102: ---\n103: \n104: ## 4. WHO STILL SPEAKS THE OLD CONTRACT\n105: \n106: Before changing anything shared, enumerate what was built against the old shape:\n107: \n108: - Deployed servers and running processes still on the old code.\n109: - Installed clients, SDKs, and other repositories that pin this interface.\n110: - Caches, queues, and in-flight messages holding the old format.\n111: - **Persisted data written by the old code:** the most-missed one.\n112: - Saved configuration, environment files, and CI definitions.\n113: - Documentation and examples that will now be wrong.\n114: \n115: Name the ones that exist here. \"No other callers\" is a claim under\n116: `evidence-and-proof.md`, grep for it, do not assume it.\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 5. PERSISTENCE BOUNDARIES\n121: \n122: **Removing a file from the working tree is not eradicating its content.** These are\n123: different jobs and the operator means one of them:\n124: \n125: | Boundary | Where the content still lives |\n126: |----------|-------------------------------|\n127: | Working tree | the file itself |\n128: | Local repository | index, stashes, prior commits, reflog |\n129: | Remote | pushed branches, tags, PRs, forks, CI logs |\n130: | Derived | build artifacts, caches, coverage reports, bundles |\n131: | External | logs, backups, monitoring, anything already sent |\n132: \n133: Inventory every location that applies, then **keep ordinary removal scoped to the\n134: surface that was asked for**. Do not rewrite history, branches, or reflogs to \"finish\n135: the job\" until the rollback is written and the operator has approved that specific\n136: destructive step. If a secret was committed, say so plainly, rotation is the real\n137: remedy and it is the operator's to run.\n138: \n139: ---\n140: \n141: ## 6. INSTALLING IS A MUTATION\n142: \n143: A dependency install changes the environment, the lockfile, and the trust surface, and\n144: it is the one mutation that feels like a read. Same gates as any other change: in scope,\n145: is there an existing tool, what is the rollback. Prefer what the project already has.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 7. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Placed the action on the reversibility ladder and said so.\n152: - [ ] Looked at the target before deleting or overwriting it.\n153: - [ ] The rollback sentence is written and concrete.\n154: - [ ] For tier 3, I have an explicit, in-the-moment yes for *this* action.\n155: - [ ] Enumerated what still speaks the old contract, by searching rather than assuming.\n156: - [ ] Removal is scoped to the requested surface; nothing wider happened silently.\n157: - [ ] Cheap, reversible forks were decided rather than deliberated.\n158: - [ ] Any installation went through the same scope, approval and verification as any other mutation."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794543805700,"end":1794543806437}}}}
